    "rationale": "Targeted approximately 2,000 positive training images per detected class (person, dog, cat) using a coherent backbone dataset family. The Open Images (openimages_challenge_2019_det_train) dataset provides strong, broad coverage for all three classes and is chosen as the primary backbone. To maintain diversity while preserving a shared backbone, I allocated: - person: 1,500 from Open Images + 500 from COCO (coco2017_det_train) for a total of 2,000 training images; - dog: 1,200 from Open Images + 600 from COCO + 200 from LVIS (LVIS_det_train) for a total of 2,000; - cat: 1,500 from Open Images + 500 from COCO for a total of 2,000. All selections are from training sources only. The per-class pools thus are balanced (2,000 each) with a diverse mix that still emphasizes a common backbone (Open Images), satisfying the guideline to prefer a shared backbone across classes while allowing limited diversification (COCO and LVIS). Approximate total training pool across all classes: ~6,000 images; this stays well within the suggested per-class (10,000) and total (50,000) allocation ceilings. No explicit download budget was provided, so the chosen counts were not budget-constrained. Domain alignment is achieved by using general-object datasets commonly used for detection benchmarks (Open Images, COCO, LVIS).",
